{
  "dom_id": "step-q65209",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "At which points on the curve $3y{x}^{2}=48+3x{y}^{2}$ is the tangent vector to the curve perpendicular to the vector $i-j?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{x}^{2} & =48+3x{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y{x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2} & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=3y{x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=48.$ \n\nA tangent vector to the level curve can be computed as follows:\n\n$$\nt(x,y)={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j\n$$\n\nSo, we calculate $t(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y) & ={f}_{y}(x,y)\\,i-{f}_{x}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3y{x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2})i-\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3y{x}^{2}-3x{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(3{x}^{2}-6xy)i-(6xy-3{y}^{2})j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince our tangent vector needs to be perpendicular to $i$, we must have $t\\cdot (i-j)=0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t(x,y)\\cdot (i-j) & =0 \\\\ ((3{x}^{2}-6xy)i-(6xy-3{y}^{2})j)\\cdot (i-j) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-6xy+6xy-3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ (x-y)(x+y) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=y$ or $x=-y.$ Substituting $x=y$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{x}^{2} & =48+3x{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y{y}^{2} & =48+3y{y}^{2} \\\\ 3{y}^{3} & =48+3{y}^{3} \\\\ 0 & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we get a false statement, we conclude that does not exist points of the form $(x,x)$ on the curve. Substituting $x=-y$ into the equation of our curve gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y{x}^{2} & =48+3x{y}^{2} \\\\ 3y(-y)^{2} & =48+3(-y){y}^{2} \\\\ 3{y}^{3} & =48-3{y}^{3} \\\\ 6{y}^{3} & =48 \\\\ {y}^{3} & =8 \\\\ y & =\\sqrt[3]{8} \\\\ y & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally:\n\n- If $y=2$, we get $x=-2$ and the corresponding point is $(-2,2).$",
